The Charakuru vs. our pipeline research subagent has completed. Key takeaways:

1. Charakuru handles shape contracts (separate head/body, 4-direction, T-pose, same scale), Tripo candidates, visual parting, body-weight transfer, JSON springs, and VRM auto-checks at the source level; humans only confirm appearance at 6 checkpoints. We patch downstream at the animation and prop layer (constant rotation for hammer/shield, grip correction, tail weight masks).

2. Charakuru has no prop attachment points, 105 px top-down acceptance, or combat timing — we must keep those ourselves. The document does not mention whether it works for big-headed, short-limbed animal puppets.

3. The SPEC premise "motion-B selected" is not supported by evidence: motion-B/REPORT.md:3 says the owner's visual acceptance is pending, and HANDOVER.md:77 says three paths are still to be decided.

4. License: LICENSE:233–236 permits the donor to be used only for local fitting and weight transfer, not for separate distribution. Whether exported weights can be shipped with the game needs confirmation from the owner/author; I did not reach a legal conclusion.

5. Tassel: Charakuru's approach is to separate the whole piece, fix the root, add end points, and not set a bone at the center. Our head ornament is a rigid prop attached to slot.head, not passing through its weight/spring pipeline; the tassel needs a separate attachment bone (inferred).

6. Validation: sources.json has 60 entries; the script verified that all quotes exist verbatim at the marked lines, and 3 random entries were checked with sed.

7. REPORT.md was not written: the call to write the report file was rejected (subagents can only return text). The full report was not persisted; the dispatcher should decide how to save it.

